**Analysis:** The difference between the **Base Case** and the **Best Case** underscores the critical interplay between residence time and membrane area. In the Base Case (GHSV = 5000 h-1, O_M/V_r = 26.67 m-1), the reactor is heavily *permeation-limited*: only {{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Base Case']['Water_Removal']) }}% of the produced water is removed. Decreasing GHSV to 500 h-1 increases the contact time tenfold, while scaling up the membrane area to 133.33 m-1 multiplies the extraction surface by 5x. Together, water removal spikes to {{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['Water_Removal']) }}%, yielding a {{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['X_MR']) }}% conversion (+{{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['Gain_X']) }} pp over TR). Adding a 99-bar trans-membrane pressure driving force further accelerates Maxwell-Stefan extraction, pushing conversion to {{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case + dP 99 bar']['X_MR']) }}%.

**Source Paper Discrepancy:** Hauth et al. 2025 lists O_M/V_r = 133.3 m-1 in Figure 12 but 26.67 m-1 in the final text for the "best case." We simulate both and document the discrepancy. This single parameter is responsible for the massive difference in water removal ({{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Text Discrepancy)']['Water_Removal']) }}% vs {{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['Water_Removal']) }}%).

