## 10. Conclusions

1. **Membrane reactors break the equilibrium ceiling.** The traditional CO2 hydrogenation reactor is capped at {{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['X_TR']) }}% conversion (250 C, 100 bar, GHSV 500). By integrating a water-selective NaA zeolite membrane, the membrane reactor achieves {{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['X_MR']) }}% (+{{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['Gain_X']) }} pp), representing a {{ "%0.1f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['Gain_X'] / results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['X_TR'] * 100) }}% relative improvement.

2. **The dominant intensification lever is membrane area-to-volume ratio (O_M/V_r).** Increasing O_M/V_r from 26.67 to 133.33 m-1 (5x) while holding all other parameters constant increases water removal from {{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['GHSV 500']['Water_Removal']) }}% to {{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['Water_Removal']) }}% and the conversion gain from +{{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['GHSV 500']['Gain_X']) }} pp to +{{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['Gain_X']) }} pp. This is confirmed by both the physics model and the ML feature importance ranking.

3. **Space velocity (GHSV) is the second critical factor.** Reducing GHSV from 5000 to 500 h-1 increases the gas residence time from ~0.72 s to ~7.2 s, allowing the membrane to extract a meaningful fraction of the produced water. Without sufficient residence time, even a large membrane area cannot compensate (High Area case: only {{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['High Area']['Water_Removal']) }}% removal at GHSV 5000).

4. **Trans-membrane pressure provides marginal but real additional gains.** Applying a 99-bar dP (permeate at 1 bar vs retentate at 100 bar) pushes the driving force for Maxwell-Stefan diffusion to its maximum, increasing conversion from {{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['X_MR']) }}% to {{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case + dP 99 bar']['X_MR']) }}% (+{{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case + dP 99 bar']['X_MR'] - results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['X_MR']) }} pp additional).

5. **The ML surrogate (ExtraTrees, R2 = 0.947-0.974) validates the physics.** Across 500 DOE cases spanning a wide operating envelope, the trained surrogate reproduces the physics engine outputs with MAPE below 9%, confirming that the governing equations are self-consistent and that no unphysical regime changes occur within the simulated design space.

6. **For ammonia decomposition, H2 extraction via Pd membrane raises conversion from {{ "%0.2f"|format(results_b['Ammonia Decomposition Base']['X_TR']) }}% to {{ "%0.2f"|format(results_b['Jiang 2021 Target']['X_MR']) }}%** (+{{ "%0.2f"|format(results_b['Jiang 2021 Target']['Gain_X']) }} pp) when the specific membrane area is increased from 0.33 to 4.5 cm2/g. This demonstrates that the extractor principle is universal across both exothermic product removal (H2O in Module A) and endothermic product removal (H2 in Module B).

